---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import MasonXMonogram from '../../components/MasonXMonogram.astro';
import TitleBlock from '../../components/TitleBlock.astro';

const title = "Stop Guessing Your Half Block Order | TMM Blog";
const description = "The 20% rule works until it doesn't. Today's projects mix bullnose, split-face, colors, and multiple widths — and 'halves' isn't one line item anymore.";
const canonical = "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/stop-guessing-half-block-order/";

const blogPostingSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "Stop Guessing Your Half Block Order",
  "description": description,
  "image": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/assets/og-image.png",
  "datePublished": "2026-07-17",
  "dateModified": "2026-07-17",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "Ryan Shanley",
    "jobTitle": "Founder",
    "worksFor": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Powder River Masonry" },
    "url": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/stop-guessing-half-block-order/",
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "The Masonry Modeler",
    "url": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/",
  },
  "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": canonical },
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Field Notes", "item": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Stop Guessing Your Half Block Order", "item": canonical },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(blogPostingSchema)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <MasonXMonogram />
  <TitleBlock sheet="B1.5 — Stop Guessing Your Half Block Order" ctaLabel="Join Waitlist →" ctaHref="/#waitlist" />

  <div class="section-divider">
    <span>Field Notes · B1.5</span>
    <span class="scale-marks"></span>
    <span>The Order</span>
  </div>

  <section class="blog-post-hero">
    <div class="blog-post-hero-inner">
      <div class="post-kicker">Field Notes</div>
      <h1>
        Stop Guessing Your Half
        <span class="accent">Block Order</span>
      </h1>
      <div class="post-meta">
        <span><strong>Ryan Shanley</strong></span>
        <span>July 2026</span>
        <span>4 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="blog-post-body">

    <p>
      If you've estimated masonry for very long, you've probably used the same rule of thumb most
      of us have. Count your stretchers. Add about 20% for halves and corners. Order the block.
    </p>

    <p>
      For decades, that worked reasonably well. But commercial masonry projects have changed.
    </p>

    <h2>The 20% rule was never really counting halves</h2>

    <p>
      The extra 20% wasn't based on an exact calculation. It was an allowance that usually covered
      regular half blocks, corners, waste, and a few field changes. On a straightforward project,
      you were probably close enough.
    </p>

    <p>
      The problem is today's projects aren't always straightforward.
    </p>

    <h2>Not all half blocks are the same</h2>

    <p>
      Walk through a set of drawings today and you might need regular halves, bullnose halves,
      double bullnose, split-face versions, multiple block widths, multiple colors. Now your
      supplier isn't asking, <em>"How many half blocks do you need?"</em> They're asking:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        How many 8-inch split-face bullnose halves? How many double bullnose? How many smooth
        regular halves?
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Those aren't interchangeable. Order the wrong units and you're making another phone call.
    </p>

    <h2>The actual list of halves you might need on one job</h2>

    <p>
      This is what "20% for halves" is really trying to cover. Every one of these is a separate
      SKU that ships on its own pallet:
    </p>

    <p><strong>By profile.</strong></p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Regular half</strong> — smooth face, no bullnose. Interior conditions, non-exposed ends.</li>
      <li><strong>Bullnose half (single)</strong> — one corner rounded. Exposed wall ends, top of parapets, corners next to openings.</li>
      <li><strong>Double bullnose half</strong> — both ends rounded. End of a return wall, free-standing walls, anywhere both sides are visible.</li>
      <li><strong>Return / jamb half</strong> — one end finished for the jamb condition at doors and windows.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>By finish.</strong> Every profile above can come in any of these:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Smooth</li>
      <li>Split-face</li>
      <li>Ground face (polished / burnished)</li>
      <li>Scored (single, double, or triple score)</li>
      <li>Any specialty finish the architect specified — glazed, honed, custom</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>By width.</strong></p>

    <ul>
      <li>4" nominal (bond course, veneer)</li>
      <li>6" nominal</li>
      <li>8" nominal (standard)</li>
      <li>10" nominal</li>
      <li>12" nominal</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Then multiply by color.</strong> A single job with a banded elevation might have
    three colors running through the wall — each color gets its own version of every SKU above.</p>

    <p>
      Do the math. Four profiles × five finishes × five widths × three colors = 300 possible
      combinations. Even a modest specialty project shakes out to 20 or 30 distinct SKUs when you
      actually count. A 20% allowance doesn't tell you which 20% you're ordering.
    </p>

    <h2>The real cost isn't the block</h2>

    <p>
      A half block isn't expensive. Waiting three weeks for a special order is. Paying freight
      to rush a pallet is. Having a crew standing around because one specialty unit is missing is.
      That's where estimating mistakes become expensive.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this matters</h2>

    <p>
      Most estimating software still treats specialty units as percentages. It doesn't understand
      why a half block exists. It doesn't know about exposed wall ends, bullnose corners, bond
      patterns, window returns, door jambs, or free-standing walls. It just adds an allowance.
    </p>

    <h2>There's a better way</h2>

    <p>
      At The Masonry Modeler we don't estimate specialty units with percentages. The software
      looks at the actual wall geometry. It knows where the wall begins and ends. It knows where
      openings occur. It understands bond patterns and exposed corners. From that, it determines
      exactly where regular halves, bullnose halves, and other specialty units belong.
    </p>

    <p>
      That's a very different approach than adding 20%. The goal isn't to replace the estimator's
      judgment — it's to eliminate one more thing you have to guess. The
      <a href="/cmu-block-count/">block-by-block CMU count</a> becomes a real list of SKUs, sorted
      the way the supplier ships them.
    </p>

    <a href="/#waitlist" class="post-cta-inline">Join the waitlist →</a>

  </article>

  <div class="blog-post-footer">
    <a href="/blog/" class="blog-back-link">← All posts</a>
  </div>

</Layout>
